// Psychology Units 3 & 4 – Practice Exam 2 (VCAA format). All scenarios and questions are original.
// Correct options are authored first; VCE.finalize() rebalances answer positions at load.

VCE.stimuli('psychology', {
  'e2-keeper': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 1–3.</em></p><p>During a penalty shoot-out, goalkeeper Ava watches the striker's hips, decides to dive to her left and saves the ball. As she lands, her elbow hits a sharp stone and she jerks her arm away before she notices any pain. Her heart pounds throughout the shoot-out. Afterwards she sits quietly on the bench and her heart rate gradually returns to normal.</p>` },
  'e2-synapse': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 4–6.</em></p><p>Neuron 1 releases neurochemical X from its axon terminals into the synaptic gap. Neurochemical X binds to receptor sites on Neuron 2, and Neuron 2 becomes <strong>more</strong> likely to fire. In the researchers' experiment, repeated release of X over several days led to the connection between Neuron 1 and Neuron 2 becoming stronger.</p>` },
  'e2-lena': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 9–15.</em></p><p>Lena, 16, moved with her family from Brazil to Melbourne. On her first day at her new school, her heart pounded and she had "butterflies" in her stomach. Over the next few weeks she stayed tense but managed her classes. She thinks, "My English isn't good enough to make friends here," and has started eating lunch alone in the library to avoid conversations. Four months later she has frequent headaches, poor sleep and keeps catching colds.</p>` },
  'e2-corr': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 16–18.</em></p><p>Researchers studied 240 Year 12 students across Victoria. Schools were grouped as government, Catholic and independent, and students were selected from each group in the same proportions as in the Victorian Year 12 population. Each student completed a questionnaire measuring coping flexibility (0–50) and a mental wellbeing scale (0–100). Students with higher coping flexibility scores tended to have higher wellbeing scores.</p>`,
  },
  'e2-cat': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 19–22.</em></p><p>Each evening, Oscar opens a squeaky pantry door to get his cat Mochi's food, and then feeds her straight away. After a few weeks, Mochi begins to salivate whenever she hears the pantry door squeak, even if Oscar is only getting cereal.</p><p>Mochi has also started meowing loudly outside Oscar's bedroom at 5 am. Oscar gets up and feeds her so that the meowing stops. Mochi now meows at 5 am every morning, and Oscar gets up straight away when she starts.</p>` },
  'e2-gba': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Question 1.</em></p><p>Researchers recruited 120 adolescents aged 13–17 from three Melbourne secondary schools. Each participant provided a stool sample, which was analysed for gut microbiota diversity, and completed a self-report anxiety questionnaire (0–40; higher = more anxiety). Participants were then grouped by microbiota diversity.</p>
<table><tr><th>Gut microbiota diversity</th><th>Number of participants</th><th>Mean anxiety score</th></tr><tr><td>Low</td><td>38</td><td>24.6</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>45</td><td>18.2</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>37</td><td>13.9</td></tr></table>`,
  },
  'e2-para': { html: `<p>Dan is a paramedic. At work he regularly attends serious accidents, and he often feels exhausted from 12-hour shifts. He also worries constantly that he will make a mistake that costs a life. When a call-out comes through, his heart races and his breathing quickens. At home he usually copes by talking problems through with his partner, but after a recent difficult call she was overseas, so he started going for long runs and writing in a journal instead.</p>` },
  'e2-class': { html: `<p>Every Friday, Mr Lowe begins the Year 9 maths test by saying "Clear your desks." Tia finds maths tests stressful: during each test her heart races and her hands sweat. After several weeks, Tia's heart races and her hands sweat whenever any teacher says "Clear your desks," even in art class.</p><p>Mr Lowe also gives a "homework pass" card to every student who scores 80% or more on the Friday test. The number of students scoring 80% or more has increased.</p>` },
  'e2-ray': { html: `<p>Ray, 72, was recently diagnosed with early Alzheimer's disease. A brain scan showed shrinkage of his hippocampus. Ray struggles to remember family events from the past few years, and when his daughter asks how he pictures her upcoming wedding, he can't describe anything. However, he can still play the guitar fluently, a skill he learnt as a teenager.</p>` },
  'e2-life': {
    html: `<p>The graphs below show typical sleep at different ages.</p>`,
    charts: [
      { type: 'bar', title: 'Typical total sleep per 24 hours', xLabel: 'Age group', yLabel: 'Hours', categories: ['Newborn', 'Child (6)', 'Adolescent (16)', 'Adult (40)', 'Older adult (75)'], yMax: 18, yStep: 3, series: [{ name: 'Hours', values: [16, 10.5, 9, 7.75, 7] }] },
      { type: 'bar', title: 'Proportion of sleep spent in REM (%)', xLabel: 'Age group', yLabel: '% of sleep in REM', categories: ['Newborn', 'Child (6)', 'Adolescent (16)', 'Adult (40)', 'Older adult (75)'], yMax: 60, yStep: 10, series: [{ name: '% REM', values: [50, 25, 22, 22, 18] }] },
    ],
    source: 'Values are approximate typical figures.',
  },
  'e2-tahlia': { html: `<p>Tahlia, 17, is in Year 12, captain of her basketball team and works two shifts a week at a café. At the start of Term 3 she was managing well. Over the term, after a falling out with close friends and a run of poor SAC results, she stopped going to training, cancelled most of her shifts and began finding it hard to get out of bed on school days. Her teammate Jaylee, a proud Wiradjuri young woman, notices and invites Tahlia to join her family's weekend camping trips.</p>` },
  'e2-rt': {
    html: `<p>Researchers tested whether one night of full sleep deprivation slows reaction times. Ten university student volunteers completed a driving-simulator braking test once after a normal night's sleep and once after being kept awake for 24 hours in the laboratory. Half did the normal-sleep condition first and half did the sleep-deprived condition first. Mean braking reaction times (milliseconds) are shown.</p>
<table><tr><th>Participant</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr>
<tr><td>Normal sleep (ms)</td><td>412</td><td>398</td><td>455</td><td>430</td><td>401</td><td>420</td><td>445</td><td>418</td><td>409</td><td>436</td></tr>
<tr><td>24 hours awake (ms)</td><td>540</td><td>515</td><td>602</td><td>571</td><td>533</td><td>560</td><td>1490</td><td>548</td><td>529</td><td>577</td></tr></table>
<p>During the 24 hours awake, Participant 7 reported falling asleep briefly at the wheel during the test.</p>`,
  },
  'e2-er': {
    html: `<p>Sam, 24, has a specific phobia of dogs. He was bitten by a dog at age eight and has avoided dogs ever since, crossing the road when he sees one and refusing to visit friends who own dogs. Sam's mother has experienced high anxiety for most of her life. When Sam pictures a dog, he imagines it "lunging for my throat". His friends sometimes tease him about his fear, and for years he refused to see a psychologist because "people will think I'm weak".</p>
<p>Sam finally began systematic desensitisation with a psychologist. At the start of each weekly session he rated his fear of the item at the <strong>top</strong> of his fear hierarchy (patting a dog) from 0 (no fear) to 100 (extreme fear).</p>`,
    chart: { type: 'line', title: "Sam's fear rating for patting a dog", xLabel: 'Session', yLabel: 'Fear rating (0–100)', categories: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'], yMax: 100, yStep: 20, series: [{ name: 'Fear rating', values: [95, 92, 85, 78, 66, 55, 41, 30] }] },
  },
});

const E2 = (n) => `e2-a${String(n).padStart(2, '0')}`;

VCE.questions('psychology', [
  // ============================================================ SECTION A
  { id: E2(1), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div'], stimulus: 'e2-keeper',
    stem: '<p>Which of the following best describes Ava\'s dive to her left?</p>', header: ['Type of response', 'Motor messages carried by'],
    options: [['conscious', 'the somatic nervous system'], ['unconscious', 'the somatic nervous system'], ['conscious', 'the autonomic nervous system'], ['unconscious', 'the sympathetic nervous system']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Did Ava decide to dive? Which division moves skeletal muscles?',
    why: ['Ava decided to dive, which is a conscious, voluntary response; the somatic nervous system carries motor messages from the CNS to the skeletal muscles that move her.', 'The dive was a decision, so it was conscious, not unconscious.', 'The autonomic nervous system controls organs and glands, not voluntary skeletal movement.', 'The dive was deliberate (conscious), and the sympathetic division does not control voluntary skeletal movement.'] },
  { id: E2(2), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div'], stimulus: 'e2-keeper',
    stem: '<p>When Ava jerked her arm away from the stone, which neuron in her spinal cord passed the message from the sensory neuron to the motor neuron?</p>',
    options: ['an interneuron', 'a sensory neuron in the brain', 'a parasympathetic neuron', 'a neuron of the enteric nervous system'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'This was a spinal reflex. What connects sensory and motor neurons in the spinal cord?',
    why: ['In a spinal reflex, an interneuron in the spinal cord relays the message from the sensory neuron directly to the motor neuron, without the brain initiating the response.', 'The brain is not involved in initiating a spinal reflex.', 'Parasympathetic neurons calm the body; they are not part of the reflex arc.', 'The enteric nervous system controls the gut, not reflexes of the arm.'] },
  { id: E2(3), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div', 'st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-keeper',
    stem: '<p>Ava\'s heart rate gradually returning to normal on the bench is mainly due to</p>',
    options: ['the parasympathetic nervous system restoring the body to a state of balance.', 'the sympathetic nervous system becoming dominant.', 'the somatic nervous system slowing her heart.', 'a spinal reflex initiated by her spinal cord.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Which branch of the autonomic nervous system calms the body after a threat?',
    why: ['Once the threat has passed, the parasympathetic nervous system becomes dominant, slowing heart rate and returning the body to homeostasis.', 'Sympathetic dominance would keep her heart rate high.', 'The somatic system controls skeletal muscles, not the heart.', 'A gradual return to resting heart rate is autonomic regulation, not a spinal reflex.'] },
  { id: E2(4), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'], stimulus: 'e2-synapse',
    stem: '<p>Neurochemical X is most likely</p>',
    options: ['glutamate, because it has an excitatory effect.', 'GABA, because it has an excitatory effect.', 'GABA, because it has an inhibitory effect.', 'serotonin, because it acts only at a single synapse.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'X makes the post-synaptic neuron more likely to fire, and the connection strengthened.',
    why: ['X made Neuron 2 more likely to fire (excitatory), and repeated release strengthened the connection. This fits glutamate, the main excitatory neurotransmitter, which is involved in long-term potentiation.', 'GABA is inhibitory, not excitatory.', 'GABA is inhibitory, which would make Neuron 2 less likely to fire, the opposite of the result.', 'Serotonin is a neuromodulator acting across widespread areas, not a single synapse.'] },
  { id: E2(5), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'], stimulus: 'e2-synapse',
    stem: '<p>The receptor sites that neurochemical X binds to are most likely located on</p>',
    options: ['the dendrites of Neuron 2.', 'the axon terminals of Neuron 2.', 'the synaptic vesicles of Neuron 1.', 'the dendrites of Neuron 1.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'X travels from the pre-synaptic neuron to the post-synaptic neuron.',
    why: ['Neurotransmitters released from the pre-synaptic neuron (Neuron 1) bind to receptor sites on the dendrites of the post-synaptic neuron (Neuron 2).', 'Axon terminals release neurotransmitters; they are not where incoming neurotransmitters bind in this pathway.', 'Synaptic vesicles store the neurotransmitter inside Neuron 1; they are not receptors.', 'Neuron 1 is the pre-synaptic neuron sending the message.'] },
  { id: E2(6), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-plast'], stimulus: 'e2-synapse',
    stem: '<p>The strengthening of the connection between Neuron 1 and Neuron 2 after repeated release of X is best described as</p>',
    options: ['long-term potentiation.', 'long-term depression.', 'pruning.', 'neuromodulation.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Repeated stimulation leading to a long-lasting stronger synapse.',
    why: ['Long-term potentiation is the long-lasting strengthening of a synaptic connection following repeated high-intensity stimulation.', 'Long-term depression weakens synapses.', 'Pruning removes unused connections.', 'Neuromodulation alters the responsiveness of many neurons; it does not describe a single synapse strengthening through repeated use.'] },
  { id: E2(7), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'],
    stem: '<p>Compared with a neurotransmitter such as GABA, a neuromodulator such as dopamine</p>',
    options: ['acts on a large group of neurons across a brain region, altering how responsive they are to other neurotransmitters.', 'acts only at one synapse and always has an inhibitory effect.', 'does not need to bind to receptor sites.', 'is released only by sensory neurons.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Think "widespread" and "responsiveness".',
    why: ['Neuromodulators act on many neurons across widespread brain areas, increasing or decreasing their responsiveness to neurotransmitter signals.', 'Acting at a single synapse describes a neurotransmitter, and dopamine is not always inhibitory.', 'Neuromodulators still bind to specific receptor sites.', 'Dopamine is released by neurons in the brain, not only by sensory neurons.'] },
  { id: E2(8), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-plast'],
    stem: '<p>Which statement about long-term depression (LTD) is correct?</p>',
    options: ['It results from repeated low-intensity stimulation and weakens synaptic connections.', 'It occurs only when neurons stop firing completely.', 'It strengthens synaptic connections through sprouting.', 'It prevents learning and memory from occurring.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'LTD is not "no stimulation".',
    why: ['LTD is a long-lasting weakening of synaptic connections caused by repeated low-intensity (low-frequency) stimulation.', 'LTD involves low-level stimulation, not a complete absence of firing.', 'Sprouting and strengthening are associated with LTP.', 'LTD supports learning by weakening unneeded connections, working together with LTP.'] },
  { id: E2(9), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>Lena\'s pounding heart and "butterflies" on her first day are best described as</p>',
    options: ['a physiological response to acute stress, involving sympathetic nervous system activity.', 'a psychological response to chronic stress, involving cortisol.', 'a parasympathetic freeze response.', 'a spinal reflex to an external stressor.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Short-term, intense, bodily changes.',
    why: ['A pounding heart and stomach changes are physiological responses; on the first day the stress is short-term (acute), driven by sympathetic activation (fight-or-flight).', 'These are physiological (not psychological) responses, and the first day is acute, not chronic, stress.', 'Freeze involves immobility; nothing suggests Lena froze.', 'Stress responses are not spinal reflexes.'] },
  { id: E2(10), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>Four months later, Lena\'s headaches, poor sleep and frequent colds are most likely linked to</p>',
    options: ['chronic stress and prolonged elevated cortisol.', 'acute stress and a single release of adrenaline.', 'parasympathetic dominance for four months.', 'a reduction in cortisol caused by eating alone.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Long-term stress, and a hormone that suppresses the immune system over time.',
    why: ['Prolonged (chronic) stress keeps cortisol elevated, which over time suppresses the immune system (frequent colds) and is linked to headaches and sleep problems.', 'A single adrenaline release relates to acute stress and would not cause months of symptoms.', 'Parasympathetic dominance would calm the body.', 'Avoidance does not reduce cortisol, and the symptoms indicate higher, not lower, stress.'] },
  { id: E2(11), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gas'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>According to the General Adaptation Syndrome, during the weeks when Lena "stayed tense but managed her classes" she was most likely in the</p>', header: ['Stage', 'Resistance to stress'],
    options: [['resistance stage', 'above normal'], ['resistance stage', 'below normal'], ['exhaustion stage', 'below normal'], ['alarm reaction (shock)', 'below normal']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'She is still coping, but the stressor hasn\'t gone away.',
    why: ['In the resistance stage the body adapts to an ongoing stressor; cortisol keeps arousal and resistance above normal, so Lena can keep functioning while remaining tense.', 'Resistance is above, not below, normal in this stage.', 'Exhaustion comes later, when resources are depleted (e.g. her frequent illness at four months).', 'Shock happens briefly at the very start of a stressor.'] },
  { id: E2(12), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-tmsc'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>Lena\'s thought, "My English isn\'t good enough to make friends here," is best described as</p>',
    options: ['a secondary appraisal, judging that her coping resources are inadequate.', 'a primary appraisal of the situation as benign-positive.', 'a primary appraisal of the situation as irrelevant.', 'a secondary appraisal that the situation is a challenge.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Is she judging the situation itself, or her ability to cope with it?',
    why: ['Lena is evaluating her resources (her English skills) for dealing with the situation and judging them as inadequate, which is secondary appraisal.', 'Benign-positive means she sees no stress, which is not the case.', 'She clearly sees the situation as relevant.', '"Challenge" is a type of primary appraisal, not secondary.'] },
  { id: E2(13), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-coping'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>Eating lunch alone in the library to avoid conversations is best evaluated as</p>',
    options: ['an avoidance strategy that may reduce anxiety in the short term but is unlikely to help Lena make friends.', 'an approach strategy that directly addresses her stressor.', 'an example of coping flexibility.', 'an avoidance strategy that is the most effective long-term option in this context.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Does this strategy confront or evade the stressor? What happens long-term?',
    why: ['Avoiding conversations evades the stressor; it may ease anxiety briefly but prevents Lena from building friendships or language confidence, so it lacks context-specific effectiveness long-term.', 'Avoiding conversations is not approaching the stressor.', 'Coping flexibility is recognising a strategy isn\'t working and changing it; Lena has not done this.', 'Avoidance here is unlikely to be effective long-term because it maintains her isolation.'] },
  { id: E2(14), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gba'], stimulus: 'e2-lena',
    stem: '<p>Lena\'s "butterflies" show that stress can affect the gut. Which statement best describes the gut–brain axis?</p>',
    options: ['It is a bidirectional communication network between the brain and the gastrointestinal tract, mainly via the vagus nerve.', 'It carries information only from the brain to the gut.', 'It is part of the somatic nervous system.', 'It operates independently of the autonomic nervous system.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Two-way, and one particular nerve.',
    why: ['The gut–brain axis is a two-way network linking the brain and the gut, with the vagus nerve the main connection between the enteric nervous system and the CNS.', 'Communication is bidirectional, with most vagus nerve information travelling gut → brain.', 'It involves the autonomic (including enteric) nervous system, not the somatic.', 'The autonomic nervous system, including the enteric division, is part of the gut–brain axis.'] },
  { id: E2(15), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-tmsc'],
    stem: '<p>A limitation of the explanatory power of Lazarus and Folkman\'s Transactional Model of Stress and Coping is that</p>',
    options: ['appraisals are subjective and difficult to measure objectively.', 'it proposes that stress depends on the individual\'s appraisal.', 'it explains why two people can respond differently to the same stressor.', 'it includes an evaluation of coping resources.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Three options are strengths.',
    why: ['Appraisals happen in the mind and are usually measured by self-report, which makes the model hard to test objectively, a limitation of its explanatory power.', 'This is the model\'s central idea and a strength.', 'Explaining individual differences is a key strength.', 'Including secondary appraisal is a feature, not a limitation.'] },
  { id: E2(16), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-method'], stimulus: 'e2-corr',
    stem: '<p>Which investigation methodology was used?</p>',
    options: ['correlational study', 'controlled experiment with a between-subjects design', 'case study', 'literature review'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Was any variable manipulated?',
    why: ['The researchers measured two variables as they naturally occurred (coping flexibility and wellbeing) without manipulating either, which is a correlational study.', 'No independent variable was manipulated, so it is not an experiment.', 'A case study is an in-depth study of a particular person or situation, not 240 students.', 'The researchers generated primary data; a literature review uses secondary data.'] },
  { id: E2(17), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-method'], stimulus: 'e2-corr',
    stem: '<p>The sampling technique used was</p>',
    options: ['stratified sampling, which helps the sample represent the different school sectors.', 'random sampling, because every student had an equal chance of selection.', 'stratified sampling, which guarantees the results can be applied to all adolescents worldwide.', 'random allocation, which ensures the groups are equal.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Students were selected from subgroups in the same proportions as the population.',
    why: ['Dividing the population into strata (school sectors) and sampling in matching proportions is stratified sampling, which improves representativeness.', 'Selection was based on proportions of subgroups, which is stratified, not simple random sampling.', 'Stratified sampling improves representativeness of the Victorian Year 12 population, not adolescents worldwide.', 'Random allocation assigns participants to experimental groups; this study had no experimental groups.'] },
  { id: E2(18), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-conclude', 'st-coping'], stimulus: 'e2-corr',
    stem: '<p>Which conclusion is most appropriate?</p>',
    options: ['There is a positive relationship between coping flexibility and mental wellbeing among Victorian Year 12 students.', 'Increasing coping flexibility causes higher mental wellbeing.', 'High mental wellbeing causes students to become more flexible copers.', 'Coping flexibility is unrelated to mental wellbeing.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What can a correlational study show?',
    why: ['The results show an association: higher coping flexibility goes with higher wellbeing, in the population sampled.', 'A correlational design cannot establish cause and effect.', 'The direction of causation cannot be determined.', 'The results show a relationship.'] },
  { id: E2(19), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-cat',
    stem: '<p>In the classical conditioning of Mochi, which of the following is correct?</p>', header: ['Neutral stimulus', 'Conditioned response'],
    options: [['the pantry door squeak', 'salivating to the squeak'], ['the cat food', 'salivating to the squeak'], ['the pantry door squeak', 'salivating to the food'], ['Oscar', 'running to the pantry']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Which stimulus originally produced no salivation? What does it produce now?',
    why: ['Before conditioning, the squeak produced no salivation (NS). After being paired with food, the squeak alone triggers salivation, the conditioned response.', 'The food is the UCS, not a neutral stimulus.', 'Salivating to food is the unconditioned response.', 'Running is a voluntary behaviour, and the scenario describes salivation as the learned response.'] },
  { id: E2(20), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-cat',
    stem: '<p>Why did the squeak become a conditioned stimulus so quickly?</p>',
    options: ['It consistently occurred just before the food, every evening.', 'It occurred a long time after Mochi was fed.', 'Mochi chose to pay attention to it.', 'Oscar rewarded Mochi for salivating.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Order, timing and consistency.',
    why: ['Classical conditioning is most effective when the NS is presented shortly before the UCS, consistently over repeated trials, which is exactly the squeak-then-food sequence.', 'A long delay after feeding would weaken the association.', 'Classical conditioning is involuntary; the learner is passive.', 'Salivation is reflexive; it isn\'t reinforced as a voluntary behaviour.'] },
  { id: E2(21), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-cat',
    stem: '<p>For <strong>Oscar</strong>, getting up to feed Mochi has been strengthened by</p>',
    options: ['negative reinforcement, because the unpleasant meowing stops.', 'positive reinforcement, because Mochi gets food.', 'positive punishment, because he loses sleep.', 'classical conditioning, because the meowing is an unconditioned stimulus.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Focus on Oscar\'s behaviour and what happens to him afterwards.',
    why: ['Getting up and feeding Mochi removes an unpleasant stimulus (the meowing), which strengthens Oscar\'s behaviour: negative reinforcement.', 'Mochi getting food reinforces Mochi\'s meowing, not Oscar\'s getting up.', 'Punishment would decrease Oscar\'s behaviour, but he now gets up straight away.', 'Oscar\'s getting up is a voluntary behaviour shaped by its consequence: operant conditioning.'] },
  { id: E2(22), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-cat',
    stem: '<p>In the operant conditioning of <strong>Mochi\'s</strong> 5 am meowing, which of the following is correct?</p>', header: ['Behaviour', 'Consequence'],
    options: [['meowing outside the bedroom', 'food is given (positive reinforcement)'], ['meowing outside the bedroom', 'Oscar gets up (negative reinforcement)'], ['Oscar getting up', 'meowing stops (positive reinforcement)'], ['salivating', 'food is given (positive reinforcement)']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What does Mochi do, and what does she gain?',
    why: ['Mochi\'s behaviour is meowing; the consequence is food being added, which is positive reinforcement, so she meows every morning.', 'Something pleasant (food) is added for Mochi; nothing unpleasant is removed for her.', 'Oscar getting up is Oscar\'s behaviour, not Mochi\'s.', 'Salivating is a reflexive response from the classical conditioning, not the operant behaviour.'] },
  { id: E2(23), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-obs'],
    stem: '<p>Kiri watches her older brother get praised by their grandmother for setting the table neatly. The next evening, Kiri sets the table the same way. Seeing her brother praised mainly influenced which stage of observational learning?</p>',
    options: ['motivation', 'attention', 'retention', 'reproduction'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Seeing someone else rewarded makes us want to copy them.',
    why: ['Seeing her brother praised (vicarious reinforcement) increases Kiri\'s motivation to perform the behaviour herself.', 'Kiri had to pay attention to learn the behaviour, but the praise most directly affected her desire to perform it.', 'Retention is storing a mental representation of the behaviour; praise influences whether she wants to do it.', 'Reproduction is having the ability to perform it; praise doesn\'t give her this ability.'] },
  { id: E2(24), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-ab'],
    stem: '<p>A young person learns about seasonal food sources by walking Country with an Elder, hearing the stories and songs attached to particular places, and helping to gather food. This best illustrates that Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing are</p>',
    options: ['relational and multimodal, with knowledge patterned on Country.', 'based on positive reinforcement from Elders.', 'focused on individual memorisation from written texts.', 'the same across all Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander groups.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Look at who, how and where the learning happens.',
    why: ['The learning is embedded in a relationship with an Elder (relational), uses stories, songs and hands-on activity (multimodal), and is tied to particular places (patterned on Country).', 'This reduces the learning to operant conditioning.', 'The learning is oral, practical and relational, not written and individual.', 'Ways of knowing differ across language and cultural groups.'] },
  { id: E2(25), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-msm'],
    stem: '<p>Participants were shown a list of 15 unrelated words once, then immediately asked to recall them in any order. Most participants recalled between 5 and 9 words. This finding is best explained by</p>',
    options: ['the limited capacity of short-term memory.', 'the unlimited capacity of sensory memory.', 'the unlimited duration of long-term memory.', 'the brief duration of echoic memory.'], answer: 0,
    hint: '"Seven plus or minus two".',
    why: ['Short-term memory holds about 7 ± 2 items, which matches participants recalling between 5 and 9 words.', 'Sensory memory\'s large capacity doesn\'t explain the limit observed.', 'LTM is not the limiting factor in immediate recall of a list seen once.', 'The words were seen, not heard, and the limit reflects STM capacity.'] },
  { id: E2(26), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-msm'],
    stem: '<p>According to the Atkinson–Shiffrin multi-store model, information moves from sensory memory into short-term memory when</p>',
    options: ['it is attended to.', 'it is chunked.', 'it is rehearsed for more than 30 seconds.', 'it is retrieved from long-term memory.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What determines which sensory information we become aware of?',
    why: ['Only sensory information that is attended to is transferred into short-term memory; the rest decays.', 'Chunking increases STM capacity once information is already in STM.', 'Rehearsal maintains information in STM and helps transfer it to LTM.', 'Retrieval moves information from LTM to STM, not from sensory memory.'] },
  { id: E2(27), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>Which brain region is most involved in consolidating a new explicit memory and transferring it to the neocortex for long-term storage?</p>',
    options: ['hippocampus', 'cerebellum', 'basal ganglia', 'amygdala'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'This region is affected early in Alzheimer\'s disease.',
    why: ['The hippocampus encodes and consolidates new explicit memories and helps transfer them to the neocortex for storage.', 'The cerebellum is involved in implicit procedural memories for motor skills.', 'The basal ganglia are involved in implicit habits and procedures.', 'The amygdala adds emotional significance and is involved in conditioned fear, but it does not mainly transfer memories to the neocortex.'] },
  { id: E2(28), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>Months after moving house, Mei still reaches for a light switch on the wrong side of the doorway in her new bedroom. This automatic habit is most likely an</p>',
    options: ['implicit memory involving the basal ganglia.', 'explicit episodic memory stored in the neocortex.', 'explicit semantic memory involving the hippocampus.', 'implicit memory stored in the amygdala.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'It is an unconscious, automatic habit.',
    why: ['Reaching automatically for the old switch is a habit performed without conscious recall, an implicit procedural memory involving the basal ganglia.', 'Mei isn\'t consciously recalling a past event.', 'This isn\'t knowledge of a fact.', 'The amygdala is associated with emotional (fear) conditioning, not motor habits.'] },
  { id: E2(29), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'],
    stem: '<p>When Lachlan recalls his 16th birthday, he remembers the surprise cake his friends brought out (episodic) and that he was living in Geelong at the time (semantic). This shows that retrieving autobiographical events involves</p>',
    options: ['both episodic and semantic memory.', 'episodic memory only.', 'implicit memory only.', 'semantic memory only.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Two kinds of explicit memory are mentioned.',
    why: ['Autobiographical recall combines episodic memory (the specific personal experience) and semantic memory (general facts about one\'s life).', 'The fact that he lived in Geelong is semantic knowledge.', 'Both elements are explicit (consciously recalled) memories.', 'The surprise cake moment is an episodic memory.'] },
  { id: E2(30), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'],
    stem: '<p>Brain imaging of a <em>living</em> person in the early stages of Alzheimer\'s disease is most likely to show</p>',
    options: ['shrinkage of the hippocampus.', 'enlargement of the hippocampus.', 'no change, as the disease only affects the cerebellum.', 'the absence of any neurons in the neocortex.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Where does the damage start, and what does neuron death do to brain tissue?',
    why: ['Neuron death in Alzheimer\'s disease typically begins in the hippocampus, so imaging shows hippocampal shrinkage (atrophy) early in the disease.', 'Neuron death causes shrinkage, not enlargement.', 'The hippocampus and neocortex are affected first.', 'Damage is progressive; early on, the neocortex still contains many neurons.'] },
  { id: E2(31), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-mnem'],
    stem: '<p>Which of the following is an acronym?</p>',
    options: ['"HOMES" for Lakes Huron, Ontario, Michigan, Erie and Superior', '"My Very Easy Method Just Speeds Up Naming" for the planets', 'mentally placing grocery items in rooms of your house', 'a song that names landmarks along a journey'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'An acronym forms a pronounceable word.',
    why: ['The first letters of the five lakes form the pronounceable word "HOMES", so it is an acronym.', 'This is an acrostic: the first letters start the words of a phrase.', 'This is the method of loci.', 'This describes a sung narrative such as a songline.'] },
  { id: E2(32), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-asc'],
    stem: '<p>An electro-oculograph (EOG) would record the greatest activity during</p>',
    options: ['REM sleep.', 'NREM Stage 3.', 'NREM Stage 2.', 'all stages of sleep equally.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What does the EOG measure?',
    why: ['The EOG measures eye movements; REM sleep is characterised by rapid eye movements, so EOG activity is highest then.', 'Eye movements are minimal in NREM Stage 3.', 'Eye movements are minimal in NREM Stage 2.', 'EOG activity differs clearly between REM and NREM.'] },
  { id: E2(33), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-asc'],
    stem: '<p>A sleep researcher wants to record what a child who sleepwalks actually does during the night. The most suitable measure is</p>',
    options: ['video monitoring.', 'a sleep diary completed by the child.', 'an EEG only.', 'an EMG only.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'The researcher wants observable behaviour.',
    why: ['Video monitoring records observable behaviour during sleep, such as getting up and walking around.', 'The child would be asleep during the episode and couldn\'t report it accurately.', 'An EEG shows brain waves, not what the child does.', 'An EMG shows muscle activity but not the actual behaviour.'] },
  { id: E2(34), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-reg'],
    stem: '<p>Which of the following correctly identifies the location of the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) and the gland that releases melatonin?</p>', header: ['SCN located in the', 'Melatonin released by the'],
    options: [['hypothalamus', 'pineal gland'], ['pineal gland', 'hypothalamus'], ['hypothalamus', 'adrenal gland'], ['cerebellum', 'pineal gland']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'A common exam error is to put the SCN in the pineal gland.',
    why: ['The SCN is part of the hypothalamus; it signals the nearby pineal gland, which releases melatonin.', 'These are reversed.', 'The adrenal glands release adrenaline and cortisol, not melatonin.', 'The SCN is in the hypothalamus, not the cerebellum.'] },
  { id: E2(35), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-life'],
    stem: '<p>Compared with young adults, older adults typically experience</p>',
    options: ['less NREM Stage 3 sleep and more awakenings during the night.', 'more NREM Stage 3 sleep and fewer awakenings.', 'a greater proportion of REM sleep.', 'a need for 10–11 hours of sleep.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Sleep becomes lighter with age.',
    why: ['With age, NREM Stage 3 (deep sleep) declines markedly and sleep becomes lighter, with more frequent awakenings.', 'Deep sleep decreases, not increases, with age.', 'The REM proportion is similar to or slightly lower than that of younger adults.', '10–11 hours is typical of children.'] },
  { id: E2(36), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-dep'],
    stem: '<p>Which is a cognitive effect of partial sleep deprivation?</p>',
    options: ['taking longer to solve problems in a maths test', 'yawning frequently in class', 'feeling sad for no clear reason', 'falling asleep briefly during a lecture'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Cognitive = thinking processes.',
    why: ['Problem-solving is a thinking process, so slower problem-solving is a cognitive effect.', 'Yawning is an observable behaviour (behavioural).', 'Feeling sad is an emotional (affective) effect.', 'A microsleep is a behavioural effect.'] },
  { id: E2(37), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-crsd'],
    stem: '<p>A factory worker on permanent night shift sleeps poorly during the day. The most appropriate use of bright light therapy is</p>',
    options: ['bright light during the night shift, and avoiding bright light on the way home in the morning.', 'bright light immediately before going to bed in the morning.', 'bright light only on days off.', 'avoiding all light during the night shift.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Shift the circadian rhythm so the worker is alert at work and sleepy after.',
    why: ['Bright light during the shift suppresses melatonin and shifts the circadian rhythm to match night work; avoiding light afterwards allows melatonin release for daytime sleep.', 'Bright light before sleeping would suppress melatonin and make sleep harder.', 'Light on days off alone would not realign the rhythm to the work schedule.', 'Darkness during the shift would increase sleepiness at work.'] },
  { id: E2(38), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-hyg'],
    stem: '<p>Which sleep hygiene advice is best supported by research on zeitgebers?</p>',
    options: ['Get outside into daylight soon after waking in the morning.', 'Drink a strong coffee an hour before bed to relax.', 'Use a bright tablet in bed to help you unwind.', 'Keep the bedroom as warm as possible.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Which zeitgeber helps reset the SCN each day?',
    why: ['Morning daylight is the main zeitgeber that resets the SCN, helping keep the sleep-wake cycle aligned.', 'Caffeine is a stimulant that delays sleep.', 'Blue light from screens suppresses melatonin and delays sleep.', 'A warm room interferes with the fall in core body temperature that helps sleep onset.'] },
  { id: E2(39), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-def'],
    stem: '<p>Which observation is the clearest indicator of a decline in a person\'s level of functioning?</p>',
    options: ['They have stopped going to work and are no longer able to manage household tasks.', 'They feel nervous before a job interview.', 'They feel sad for a day after their team loses a final.', 'They choose to spend a quiet weekend at home.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Levels of functioning = ability to carry out everyday tasks independently.',
    why: ['Being unable to work or manage daily tasks shows a significant reduction in the ability to function independently.', 'Pre-interview nerves are normal and don\'t impair functioning.', 'A brief, understandable emotional response doesn\'t indicate reduced functioning.', 'Choosing a quiet weekend is not a decline in functioning.'] },
  { id: E2(40), pool: 'exam2', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-int'],
    stem: '<p>Benzodiazepines reduce phobic anxiety because they</p>',
    options: ['enhance the inhibitory effect of GABA, making neurons less likely to fire.', 'block GABA receptors, increasing neural firing.', 'remove the conditioned stimulus from the environment.', 'permanently cure the phobia after one dose.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'They are GABA agonists.',
    why: ['As GABA agonists, benzodiazepines bind to GABA receptor sites and enhance GABA\'s inhibitory effect, reducing neural firing and physiological anxiety.', 'Blocking GABA would increase anxiety.', 'Medication cannot remove a stimulus from the environment.', 'Benzodiazepines are short-acting and manage symptoms; they don\'t cure the phobia.'] },

  // ============================================================ SECTION B
  // Q1 – gut–brain axis research (8)
  { id: 'e2-b1a', pool: 'exam2', label: '1a', type: 'sa', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-method'], stimulus: 'e2-gba',
    stem: '<p>Name the investigation methodology used in this study.</p>', hint: 'Were participants allocated to conditions by the researchers?',
    criteria: ['Correlational study (variables measured, not manipulated)'],
    model: '<p>A correlational study: microbiota diversity and anxiety were measured as they naturally occurred, without manipulating either variable.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling it an experiment because there are "groups". The groups were formed from measured diversity, not allocated by the researchers.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b1b', pool: 'exam2', label: '1b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gba', 'sk-data'], stimulus: 'e2-gba',
    stem: '<p>Describe the relationship shown in the results, referring to data.</p>', hint: 'As one variable goes up, what happens to the other? Quote numbers.',
    criteria: ['As gut microbiota diversity increased, mean anxiety score decreased (negative relationship)', 'Data: low diversity 24.6, medium 18.2, high 13.9'],
    model: '<p>There is a negative relationship: as gut microbiota diversity increased, mean anxiety decreased, from 24.6 in the low-diversity group to 18.2 (medium) and 13.9 (high).</p>', pitfalls: ['No data quoted.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b1c', pool: 'exam2', label: '1c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-conclude', 'st-gba'], stimulus: 'e2-gba',
    stem: '<p>A news report states: "Low gut bacteria diversity causes anxiety in teenagers." Explain why this conclusion cannot be drawn from this study.</p>', hint: 'What can correlational data not show? Could a third factor explain both?',
    criteria: ['Correlational studies show an association only and cannot establish cause and effect (direction unknown: anxiety/stress could reduce diversity, as the gut–brain axis is bidirectional)', 'Another (extraneous) variable could explain both, e.g. diet, sleep or chronic stress/cortisol'],
    model: '<p>This was a correlational study, so it shows that diversity and anxiety are related, not that one causes the other. Because the gut–brain axis is bidirectional, anxiety and chronic stress (high cortisol) could reduce microbiota diversity rather than the reverse. A third variable such as diet or sleep could also influence both.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only saying "correlation isn\'t causation" without explaining.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b1d', pool: 'exam2', label: '1d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-ethics'], stimulus: 'e2-gba',
    stem: '<p>Identify and explain how <strong>two</strong> ethical guidelines should be applied in this study, given the age of the participants and the type of data collected.</p>', hint: 'Who must consent for under-18s? What about health data?',
    criteria: ['Informed consent procedures: because participants are under 18, written consent should be obtained from a parent/guardian (with the adolescent agreeing too), after the purpose and risks are explained', 'Confidentiality: stool sample results and anxiety scores are sensitive personal health information and must be kept private, with identifying details removed', 'Explanation is applied to this study (e.g. results reported only as group means; samples stored securely); OR a valid alternative guideline such as withdrawal rights explained in context'],
    model: '<p>Informed consent procedures: because the participants are aged 13–17, a parent or guardian should give written consent after the purpose, procedures (providing a stool sample, completing a questionnaire) and any risks are explained, and the adolescents themselves should also agree. Confidentiality: microbiota results and anxiety scores are sensitive personal health information, so they must be stored securely, de-identified and reported only as group results such as means, so no participant can be identified.</p>', pitfalls: ['Naming guidelines without applying them to this study.'] },
  // Q2 – stress (9)
  { id: 'e2-b2a', pool: 'exam2', label: '2a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-para',
    stem: '<p>Identify one internal stressor and one external stressor Dan experiences.</p>', hint: 'Internal = from within Dan; external = from his environment.',
    criteria: ['Internal: constant worry that he will make a mistake (or exhaustion/fatigue)', 'External: attending serious accidents (or 12-hour shifts / call-outs)'],
    model: '<p>An internal stressor is Dan\'s constant worry that he will make a fatal mistake. An external stressor is attending serious accidents at work.</p>', pitfalls: ['Giving two external stressors.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b2b', pool: 'exam2', label: '2b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-para',
    stem: '<p>Distinguish between the fight-or-flight response and the freeze response, with reference to the dominant branch of the autonomic nervous system in each.</p>', hint: 'Which branch dominates each, and what does the body do?',
    criteria: ['Fight-or-flight: sympathetic nervous system dominant, body aroused for action, e.g. increased heart rate and breathing as in Dan\'s call-out response', 'Freeze: parasympathetic nervous system dominant, person is immobile but highly alert/tense'],
    model: '<p>In fight-or-flight, the sympathetic nervous system is dominant, preparing the body for action: heart rate and breathing increase, as when Dan receives a call-out. In the freeze response, the parasympathetic nervous system is dominant: the person becomes immobile, while still highly aroused, alert and tense.</p>', pitfalls: ['Saying freeze involves being relaxed or low arousal.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b2c', pool: 'exam2', label: '2c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-resp'], stimulus: 'e2-para',
    stem: '<p>Explain how chronic stress could affect Dan\'s physical health, referring to cortisol.</p>', hint: 'Prolonged cortisol → which body system?',
    criteria: ['Dan\'s ongoing stressors keep cortisol elevated for a prolonged period (chronic stress)', 'Prolonged cortisol suppresses the immune system, making him more vulnerable to illness/infection'],
    model: '<p>Because Dan\'s stressors are ongoing, his cortisol stays elevated over a long period. Prolonged high cortisol suppresses his immune system, so he becomes more vulnerable to illnesses such as colds and infections.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing adrenaline (acute stress) instead of cortisol.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b2d', pool: 'exam2', label: '2d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-coping'], stimulus: 'e2-para',
    stem: '<p>Explain how Dan demonstrated coping flexibility, and why his new strategies could be considered to have context-specific effectiveness.</p>', hint: 'What changed, and why did the new strategies suit the new situation?',
    criteria: ['His usual strategy (talking to his partner) was unavailable/ineffective because she was overseas', 'He recognised this and switched to different strategies (running, journalling), which is coping flexibility', 'Context-specific: these strategies didn\'t rely on his partner being present and suited this situation (e.g. journalling let him process the difficult call alone; running reduced physiological arousal)'],
    model: '<p>Dan normally copes by talking to his partner, but after this call she was overseas, so that strategy wasn\'t available. He recognised this and switched to running and journalling, which shows coping flexibility. These strategies had context-specific effectiveness because they didn\'t depend on his partner being there: journalling let him process the difficult call on his own, and running helped reduce the physical tension from the stress response.</p>', pitfalls: ['Defining coping flexibility without applying it.'] },
  // Q3 – learning (10)
  { id: 'e2-b3a', pool: 'exam2', label: '3a', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-class',
    stem: '<p>Using the three phases of classical conditioning, explain how Tia learnt to respond to the words "Clear your desks."</p>', hint: 'Before, during, after. Name NS, UCS, UCR, CS, CR.',
    criteria: ['Before: "Clear your desks" was a NS producing no response; the maths test (UCS) produced a racing heart/sweating (UCR)', 'During: "Clear your desks" was repeatedly presented just before the maths test each Friday', 'After: "Clear your desks" became a CS that alone produces a racing heart/sweaty hands (CR), even in art class'],
    model: '<p>Before conditioning, "Clear your desks" was a neutral stimulus that produced no response, while the stressful maths test (UCS) naturally produced a racing heart and sweaty hands (UCR). During conditioning, the phrase was repeatedly said just before the test every Friday. After conditioning, "Clear your desks" became a conditioned stimulus that alone triggers a racing heart and sweating (the conditioned response), even in art class.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling the test the CS.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b3b', pool: 'exam2', label: '3b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-class',
    stem: '<p>Identify the type of consequence involved in the homework pass and explain its effect on students\' behaviour.</p>', hint: 'Added or removed? Increase or decrease?',
    criteria: ['Positive reinforcement: a desirable stimulus (the homework pass) is added after scoring 80% or more', 'This increases the likelihood of the behaviour (studying/scoring highly), shown by more students scoring 80%+'],
    model: '<p>The homework pass is positive reinforcement: a desirable item is added after students score 80% or more, which strengthens the behaviour of studying for the test, as shown by the increase in students reaching 80%.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling it negative reinforcement because homework is "removed". The pass itself is something given.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b3c', pool: 'exam2', label: '3c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e2-class',
    stem: '<p>Mr Lowe considers handing out all the homework passes at the end of term instead of after each test. Explain why this is likely to make the reinforcement less effective.</p>', hint: 'Timing of consequences.',
    criteria: ['Consequences are most effective when they follow the behaviour immediately (and consistently)', 'A delay of weeks weakens the association between scoring highly on a particular test and the reward, so the behaviour is less likely to be strengthened'],
    model: '<p>Reinforcement is most effective when it immediately follows the behaviour. If passes are delayed until the end of term, students are less likely to associate the reward with their effort on each test, so the behaviour will not be strengthened as much.</p>', pitfalls: ['Not linking to timing.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b3d', pool: 'exam2', label: '3d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-ab', 'le-beh'],
    stem: '<p>Compare how learning occurs in Mr Lowe\'s homework-pass system with learning in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing.</p>', hint: 'Compare = at least one similarity or difference with linking words. Think about the learner\'s position.',
    criteria: ['Homework-pass system: behaviourist; individual learners change behaviour in response to consequences (reinforcement) from the teacher', 'Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing: the learner is situated within a system; learning is embedded in relationships (Elders, kin, community) and is multimodal and patterned on Country', 'Explicit comparison, e.g. one is individual and reward-driven whereas the other is relational and holistic (or similarity: both involve learning from others/feedback within a social context)'],
    model: '<p>In Mr Lowe\'s system, learning follows a behaviourist approach: each student is an individual whose behaviour is shaped by a consequence (the reinforcing homework pass). In contrast, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing situate the learner within a system, where learning is embedded in relationships with Elders, kin, community and Country, and knowledge is shared through multiple modes such as story, song and experience on Country. Whereas the homework pass treats learning as an individual response to rewards, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing see it as relational and holistic.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing each separately with no comparison language.'] },
  // Q4 – memory (10)
  { id: 'e2-b4a', pool: 'exam2', label: '4a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain', 'me-epi'], stimulus: 'e2-ray',
    stem: '<p>Explain why Ray can still play the guitar fluently despite his memory difficulties.</p>', hint: 'What type of memory is guitar playing, and which regions are involved?',
    criteria: ['Playing guitar is an implicit (procedural) memory', 'Implicit procedural memories involve the cerebellum and basal ganglia, which are less affected than the hippocampus in early Alzheimer\'s disease (the hippocampus is mainly involved in explicit memory)'],
    model: '<p>Playing the guitar is an implicit procedural memory, which relies mainly on the cerebellum and basal ganglia rather than the hippocampus. Early Alzheimer\'s disease mainly damages the hippocampus, which is involved in explicit memories, so Ray\'s well-learnt motor skill remains intact.</p>', pitfalls: ['Saying guitar playing is stored in the hippocampus.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b4b', pool: 'exam2', label: '4b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'], stimulus: 'e2-ray',
    stem: '<p>Explain how Alzheimer\'s disease is affecting Ray\'s ability to retrieve autobiographical events and to imagine his daughter\'s wedding. Refer to the biological changes involved.</p>', hint: 'Plaques and tangles → neuron death → hippocampus → episodic memory and future thinking.',
    criteria: ['Amyloid plaques (around neurons) and neurofibrillary tangles (within neurons) lead to neuron death, beginning in the hippocampus (consistent with his scan)', 'Damage impairs encoding/retrieval of episodic (and semantic) autobiographical memories, e.g. recent family events', 'Imagining a future event relies on recombining details from episodic/semantic memories, so with fewer accessible memories he cannot construct a possible imagined future (the wedding)'],
    model: '<p>In Alzheimer\'s disease, amyloid plaques form around neurons and neurofibrillary tangles form within them, leading to neuron death that begins in the hippocampus, consistent with the shrinkage on Ray\'s scan. This impairs his ability to encode and retrieve episodic and semantic autobiographical memories, such as family events from recent years. Because imagining the future involves recombining details from past episodic and semantic memories, Ray has few details to draw on, so he cannot construct a possible imagined future such as his daughter\'s wedding.</p>', pitfalls: ['Not linking memory of the past to imagining the future.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b4c', pool: 'exam2', label: '4c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'],
    stem: '<p>Give one advantage of brain imaging and one advantage of post-mortem studies as sources of evidence about Alzheimer\'s disease.</p>', hint: 'One works while the person is alive; the other allows direct examination of tissue.',
    criteria: ['Brain imaging: can be done on living people, so changes (e.g. shrinkage) can be tracked over time and related to their current symptoms', 'Post-mortem: brain tissue can be examined directly, confirming plaques, tangles and neuron loss'],
    model: '<p>Brain imaging can be done while the person is alive, so researchers can track changes such as hippocampal shrinkage over time and relate them to symptoms. Post-mortem studies allow direct examination of brain tissue, confirming the presence of amyloid plaques, neurofibrillary tangles and neuron loss.</p>', pitfalls: ['Naming specific scan types. You don\'t need to.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b4d', pool: 'exam2', label: '4d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-mnem', 'me-epi'], stimulus: 'e2-ray',
    stem: '<p>Ray\'s grandson suggests he use the method of loci to remember his daily medications. Explain how the method of loci works, and give one reason it may be difficult for Ray to use.</p>', hint: 'Steps of the method, then link to what Alzheimer\'s affects.',
    criteria: ['Visualise a familiar place/route and mentally place each item (medication) at a location along it', 'Retrieve by mentally walking the route, with each location acting as a retrieval cue', 'Difficulty: forming and retrieving new explicit associations relies on the hippocampus, which is damaged in Alzheimer\'s disease, so encoding new item–location links may fail (or he may struggle to construct vivid imagery)'],
    model: '<p>In the method of loci, Ray would picture a familiar place, such as his house, and mentally place each medication at a location along a route through it. To recall them, he would mentally walk the route, using each location as a retrieval cue. However, this requires encoding new explicit associations between items and locations, which depends on the hippocampus. Because Alzheimer\'s disease has damaged Ray\'s hippocampus, he may be unable to form or retrieve these new associations consistently.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing an acrostic instead.'] },
  // Q5 – sleep across the life span (11)
  { id: 'e2-b5a', pool: 'exam2', label: '5a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-life'], stimulus: 'e2-life',
    stem: '<p>Describe two trends shown in the graphs.</p>', hint: 'One trend per graph, with data.',
    criteria: ['Total sleep decreases with age, e.g. from about 16 hours (newborn) to about 7 hours (older adult)', 'Proportion of REM decreases with age, from about 50% (newborn) to about 18% (older adult), with the largest drop from infancy to childhood'],
    model: '<p>Total sleep decreases with age, from about 16 hours per day for newborns to about 7 hours for older adults. The proportion of sleep spent in REM also decreases, from about 50% in newborns to about 25% by age 6 and about 18% in older adults, with the steepest decline between infancy and childhood.</p>', pitfalls: ['Trends without data.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b5b', pool: 'exam2', label: '5b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-life'], stimulus: 'e2-life',
    stem: '<p>Explain why newborns spend a much greater proportion of their sleep in REM than older adults.</p>', hint: 'What is REM thought to support, and when is that most needed?',
    criteria: ['Newborns ~50% REM vs ~18% in older adults', 'REM sleep is thought to support brain development and the formation of synaptic connections', 'This is greatest in infancy, when the brain develops rapidly, so the demand for REM is highest then and declines as development slows'],
    model: '<p>Newborns spend about 50% of their sleep in REM compared with about 18% for older adults. REM sleep is believed to support brain development and the formation of new synaptic connections. Because the brain develops most rapidly in infancy, newborns need far more REM, and the demand declines as brain development slows with age.</p>', pitfalls: ['Explaining total sleep instead of REM proportion.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b5c', pool: 'exam2', label: '5c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-life', 'sl-reg', 'sw-dep'], stimulus: 'e2-life',
    stem: '<p>Adolescents need about 9 hours of sleep, yet many get much less on school nights. Explain a biological reason for this and one likely consequence for their functioning at school.</p>', hint: 'Melatonin timing, then an affective, behavioural or cognitive effect.',
    criteria: ['During adolescence, melatonin is released later at night (sleep phase delay), so they feel sleepy later', 'With early school start times, they wake before getting enough sleep, which is partial sleep deprivation', 'A relevant consequence, e.g. poorer concentration/attention in class (cognitive), irritability (affective), microsleeps (behavioural)'],
    model: '<p>During adolescence, the release of melatonin is delayed until later at night, so teenagers don\'t feel sleepy until late. Because school starts early, they must wake before they have had about 9 hours of sleep, so they experience partial sleep deprivation. As a result, they may have poorer concentration and attention in class, which is a cognitive effect.</p>', pitfalls: ['Blaming only phones without the biological mechanism.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b5d', pool: 'exam2', label: '5d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-asc'],
    stem: '<p>Explain how a sleep researcher could use the EEG and EMG to distinguish REM sleep from NREM Stage 3.</p>', hint: 'Describe what each tool shows in each stage.',
    criteria: ['EEG in REM: high-frequency, low-amplitude brain waves (similar to waking)', 'EEG in NREM Stage 3: low-frequency, high-amplitude brain waves', 'EMG: very low muscle activity in REM (atonia) compared with low–moderate muscle tone in NREM Stage 3'],
    model: '<p>The EEG shows high-frequency, low-amplitude brain waves during REM sleep, similar to being awake, whereas NREM Stage 3 shows low-frequency, high-amplitude waves. The EMG shows that skeletal muscle activity is almost absent during REM (muscle atonia), while some muscle tone remains in NREM Stage 3.</p>', pitfalls: ['Naming wave types (delta/beta) without describing frequency and amplitude.'] },
  // Q6 – wellbeing (11)
  { id: 'e2-b6a', pool: 'exam2', label: '6a', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-cont', 'wb-def'], stimulus: 'e2-tahlia',
    stem: '<p>Using the mental wellbeing continuum, explain how Tahlia\'s mental wellbeing has changed over Term 3. Refer to her level of functioning and to internal and external factors.</p>', hint: 'Start → now. What changed in what she does? What caused it?',
    criteria: ['At the start of term she was functioning well (managing school, sport, work), towards the mentally healthy end', 'Her level of functioning has declined: stopped training, cancelled shifts, struggling to get out of bed, so her position has moved towards the mental health problem end', 'Interaction of external factors (falling out with friends, poor SAC results) and internal factors (e.g. her thoughts about results/low mood) caused the shift, showing wellbeing fluctuates'],
    model: '<p>At the start of Term 3, Tahlia was functioning well, balancing Year 12, basketball and work, placing her towards the mentally healthy end of the continuum. Over the term her level of functioning declined: she stopped training, cancelled shifts and struggles to get out of bed, indicating a shift towards the mental health problem end. This reflects the interaction of external factors (falling out with friends and poor SAC results) with internal factors (such as low mood and negative thoughts about her results), showing that mental wellbeing fluctuates over time.</p>', pitfalls: ['Not referring to both internal and external factors.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b6b', pool: 'exam2', label: '6b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-def'], stimulus: 'e2-tahlia',
    stem: '<p>Define resilience and explain how greater resilience could have helped Tahlia during Term 3.</p>', hint: 'Definition + application.',
    criteria: ['Resilience: the ability to cope with and manage change and uncertainty (bounce back from adversity)', 'Application: greater resilience would help her cope with the friendship conflict and poor results without her functioning declining as much, recovering more quickly'],
    model: '<p>Resilience is the ability to cope with and manage change and uncertainty. With greater resilience, Tahlia might have coped with the falling out and poor SAC results by adapting, such as seeking help with her studies, rather than withdrawing from training and work, so her functioning would have recovered more quickly.</p>', pitfalls: ['Definition without application.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b6c', pool: 'exam2', label: '6c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-bps'], stimulus: 'e2-tahlia',
    stem: '<p>Using a biopsychosocial approach, suggest one protective factor from each domain that could help Tahlia maintain her mental wellbeing. Justify each.</p>', hint: 'Biological, psychological, social, each linked to Tahlia.',
    criteria: ['Biological: e.g. regular adequate sleep (or nutrition/hydration), supporting mood regulation and energy for school', 'Psychological: e.g. cognitive behavioural strategies to challenge thoughts like "I\'m failing Year 12", or mindfulness meditation to reduce rumination', 'Social: e.g. accepting Jaylee\'s invitation / reconnecting with her basketball team, giving authentic and energising support'],
    model: '<p>Biological: Tahlia should aim for regular, adequate sleep, which supports emotional regulation and the energy she needs for school and sport. Psychological: she could use cognitive behavioural strategies to challenge unhelpful thoughts about her SAC results, such as "I\'m failing everything", and replace them with realistic ones. Social: accepting Jaylee\'s invitation and reconnecting with her team would give her authentic and energising social support.</p>', pitfalls: ['Factors not linked to Tahlia.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b6d', pool: 'exam2', label: '6d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-def', 'mt-cult'], stimulus: 'e2-tahlia',
    stem: '<p>Explain why a model that considers only an individual\'s "mind and emotions" may not fully capture Jaylee\'s wellbeing as a Wiradjuri young woman.</p>', hint: 'SEWB is holistic and multidimensional. Name domains and why they are interconnected.',
    criteria: ['SEWB is a holistic, multidimensional framework for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people', 'It includes domains beyond mind and emotions, e.g. connection to body, family and kinship, community, culture, Country, spirituality and ancestors', 'The self is inseparable from and interconnected with these domains, so Jaylee\'s wellbeing depends on these connections (e.g. family camping trips may strengthen kinship and Country), which an individual mind-focused model would miss'],
    model: '<p>For Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, wellbeing is understood through social and emotional wellbeing (SEWB), a holistic and multidimensional framework. As well as mind and emotions, it includes connection to body, family and kinship, community, culture, Country, and spirituality and ancestors. Because the self is inseparable from these interconnected domains, Jaylee\'s wellbeing depends on the strength of these connections, such as time on Country with family, which a model focused only on the individual\'s mind and emotions would not capture.</p>', pitfalls: ['Listing domains without explaining interconnectedness.'] },
  // Q7 – research (11)
  { id: 'e2-b7a', pool: 'exam2', label: '7a', type: 'sa', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-method'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>Name the experimental design used.</p>', hint: 'Did each participant do both conditions?',
    criteria: ['Within-subjects design'], model: '<p>Within-subjects design, because every participant completed both conditions.</p>', pitfalls: ['Old terms such as "repeated measures".'] },
  { id: 'e2-b7b', pool: 'exam2', label: '7b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-design'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>Write a hypothesis for this experiment.</p>', hint: 'IV, DV, direction, both conditions.',
    criteria: ['Refers to both conditions (24 hours awake vs a normal night\'s sleep) and the DV (braking reaction time)', 'Direction: reaction times will be slower/longer after 24 hours awake'],
    model: '<p>University students will have slower (longer) braking reaction times on a driving simulator after 24 hours awake than after a normal night\'s sleep.</p>', pitfalls: ['No direction.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b7c', pool: 'exam2', label: '7c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-error'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>Identify the outlier in the data and explain how the researchers should deal with it.</p>', hint: 'Find the value far from the rest, then use the extra information given.',
    criteria: ['Outlier: Participant 7\'s 24-hours-awake reaction time of 1490 ms', 'Should not be automatically deleted; investigate the cause, i.e. a microsleep during the test, and report it/consider analysing with and without it (e.g. median less affected)'],
    model: '<p>Participant 7\'s reaction time of 1490 ms after 24 hours awake is an outlier, far above the others (515–602 ms). It should not simply be deleted. The researchers should investigate the cause (the participant reported falling asleep at the wheel, a microsleep), report it, and consider presenting results with and without it, or use the median, which is less affected.</p>', pitfalls: ['Simply saying "remove it".'] },
  { id: 'e2-b7d', pool: 'exam2', label: '7d', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-data', 'sk-conclude'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>Explain one reason why the results may have limited external validity.</p>', hint: 'Who were the participants, and how was driving measured?',
    criteria: ['Limitation, e.g. small sample of 10 volunteer university students / simulator rather than real driving', 'Results may not apply to other people (e.g. older or professional drivers) or to real-world driving conditions'],
    model: '<p>The sample was only 10 university student volunteers, who may not be representative of all drivers, such as older adults or truck drivers. The results may therefore not generalise to the wider population.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing internal validity instead.'] },
  { id: 'e2-b7e', pool: 'exam2', label: '7e', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-ethics'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>At hour 20, one participant became distressed and asked to go home. Name the relevant ethical guideline and explain what the researchers must do.</p>', hint: 'Can participants leave?',
    criteria: ['Withdrawal rights', 'The participant must be allowed to stop immediately without penalty (and can have their data removed); researchers should also ensure their wellbeing (e.g. not let them drive home while sleep-deprived)'],
    model: '<p>Withdrawal rights: the participant must be allowed to stop participating immediately without any penalty, and may ask for their results to be removed. Researchers should also ensure their safety, for example arranging transport rather than letting them drive home after 20 hours awake.</p>', pitfalls: ['Naming voluntary participation (which applies at the start).'] },
  { id: 'e2-b7f', pool: 'exam2', label: '7f', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-dep'], stimulus: 'e2-rt',
    stem: '<p>Relate these results to research comparing full sleep deprivation with blood alcohol concentration, and state one implication for drivers.</p>', hint: '24 hours ≈ which BAC?',
    criteria: ['About 24 hours of full sleep deprivation produces impairment comparable to a BAC of 0.10; the slower reaction times (~400 ms → ~550 ms) are consistent with this', 'Implication: driving after a night without sleep is as dangerous as driving well over the legal alcohol limit, so drivers should not drive when severely sleep-deprived'],
    model: '<p>Research shows that about 24 hours awake causes cognitive impairment comparable to a BAC of 0.10. The slower braking times after 24 hours awake (roughly 400 ms rising to over 500 ms) are consistent with this. This implies that driving after a night without sleep is as dangerous as driving at twice the legal alcohol limit, so sleep-deprived people should not drive.</p>', pitfalls: ['Using 0.05 for 24 hours.'] },
  // Q8 – extended response (10)
  { id: 'e2-b8', pool: 'exam2', label: '8', type: 'er', marks: 10, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-dev', 'ph-int', 'sk-data'], stimulus: 'e2-er',
    stem: '<p>Using a biopsychosocial approach, analyse the factors that contributed to the development and maintenance of Sam\'s specific phobia, and evaluate the effectiveness of his treatment using the data provided.</p>',
    hint: 'Biological (GABA? LTP), psychological (classical precipitation, operant perpetuation, catastrophic thinking), social (teasing, stigma). Then: what does the graph show, what are its limits?',
    criteria: [
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Biological:</strong> possible GABA dysfunction (e.g. family history of anxiety suggests reduced inhibition, so heightened fear responses), with tentative language' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Biological:</strong> LTP strengthening the neural pathway linking dogs with fear through repeated fearful encounters/thoughts' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Psychological:</strong> precipitated by classical conditioning: dog (NS) paired with bite/pain (UCS), so dogs became a CS producing fear (CR)' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Psychological:</strong> perpetuated by operant conditioning: avoidance (crossing the road) removes anxiety, so it is negatively reinforced and maintained' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Psychological:</strong> catastrophic thinking ("lunging for my throat") increases anxiety and avoidance' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Social:</strong> stigma around seeking treatment ("people will think I\'m weak") delayed help; teasing may increase distress' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Relative influence:</strong> judges which factors were most influential (e.g. conditioning precipitated and maintained it, while stigma prolonged it), showing factors interact' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Data:</strong> fear rating fell from 95 (session 1) to 30 (session 8), with the steepest fall from session 4 onwards' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Mechanism:</strong> links the improvement to systematic desensitisation (relaxation, fear hierarchy, gradual exposure, relaxation incompatible with fear)' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Evaluation:</strong> treatment appears effective but data are self-reported from one person, with no follow-up/control, and fear has not reached 0. Justified conclusion.' },
    ],
    model: `<p>Biologically, Sam's mother's long history of high anxiety suggests Sam may have inherited a tendency towards GABA dysfunction. With less neural inhibition, his fear responses may be more intense. Each time Sam encountered or thought about dogs with fear, the neural pathway linking dogs with fear was repeatedly activated, and long-term potentiation strengthened it, making the response more automatic.</p>
<p>Psychologically, the phobia was precipitated by classical conditioning: a dog (neutral stimulus) was paired with the pain of being bitten (UCS), so dogs became a conditioned stimulus that triggers fear (conditioned response). It has been perpetuated by operant conditioning: crossing the road or avoiding friends' homes removes his anxiety, which negatively reinforces avoidance, so he never learns that most dogs are safe. His catastrophic thinking ("lunging for my throat") further increases his anxiety.</p>
<p>Socially, stigma around seeking treatment ("people will think I'm weak") stopped Sam getting help for years, allowing the phobia to persist, and his friends' teasing may have increased his distress. Overall, conditioning seems the most influential factor in precipitating and maintaining the phobia, but the factors interact: stigma prolonged it and a possible biological vulnerability may have intensified it.</p>
<p>The data suggest systematic desensitisation has been effective. Sam's fear rating for patting a dog fell from 95 in session 1 to 30 by session 8, dropping fastest from session 4. This fits the therapy's mechanism: by learning relaxation and gradually working up his fear hierarchy while relaxed, a response incompatible with fear, dogs became associated with calm. However, the ratings are self-reported by one person, there is no comparison or follow-up, and his fear is still 30, so the phobia has not yet resolved. The treatment appears to be working, but continued sessions and follow-up are needed to judge its long-term effectiveness.</p>`,
    pitfalls: ['Listing factors without explaining how each contributed to Sam\'s phobia.', 'Saying GABA dysfunction is definitely present. Use tentative language ("may").', 'Describing the graph without evaluating it.', 'Forgetting the social domain.'],
  },
]);

